**LEARNING WITHOUT BARRIERS**

*A Digital Inclusion Strategy for Weston College*

Draft Framework for Discussion  |  March 2026

| *This document brings together strategic thinking, external evidence and practical actions to make Digital Inclusion a lived reality at Weston College — by design, not as an afterthought. It is intended as a discussion and debate document, not a finished plan.* |
| --- |

# **WHY THIS MATTERS: THE CASE FOR CHANGE**

### **The Policy Context**

The Post-16 Education and Skills White Paper (November 2025) sets ambitious targets for skills, digital infrastructure, and SEND inclusion — but it makes a number of significant digital assumptions that could leave our most vulnerable learners behind if we do not act proactively.

### **Key Digital Assumptions in the FE White Paper — and Their Risk at Weston College**

| **White Paper Assumption** | **Likely Risk / Reality at Weston College** |
| --- | --- |
| All learners have reliable internet access and connected devices | Coastal and rural areas around Weston-super-Mare have connectivity gaps; many ESOL and SEND learners face device poverty |
| Staff are digitally confident and can deliver effective blended/digital learning | Staff confidence with tools like MS Teams, accessible resource creation, and assistive tech varies significantly across the college |
| Digital skills are embedded across all curriculum areas | Many curriculum areas have limited digital integration; foundational skills are not consistently taught or assumed |
| SEND learners can access mainstream digital provision post-16 | Without proactive accessibility design, digital barriers actively exclude SEND learners from engaging with course materials and systems |
| Learners will engage with digital platforms for assignment submission, communication and careers | ESOL learners in particular may lack the digital literacy AND language skills to navigate VLEs, Teams, and online admin processes |
| Institutions have the infrastructure to support digital apprenticeships and T Levels in digital/AI | If foundational digital access and literacy are not in place, participation in growth sector training will be inequitable |

| *Key insight: Digital Inclusion is not mentioned explicitly enough in the White Paper — but its absence as a precondition undermines everything else the White Paper is trying to achieve.* |
| --- |

### **What DigiFest ****'****26 Told Us**

At Jisc DigiFest 2026 (From Disruption to Direction), a consistent message emerged: industries have transformed, but learning has not kept pace. The sector recognises that digital inclusion is now social inclusion — it is infrastructure, not a nice-to-have. Specifically relevant to our context:

- Only 19% of young people see real opportunities for genuine involvement in shaping digital change

- Digital poverty is real — equity of devices, connectivity, and literacy cannot be assumed

- Staff capability remains the number one barrier across FE and HE

- Ethical and responsible digital practice needs to be embedded in everyday TLA, not bolted on

# **SECTION 1: STRATEGIC VISION AND PRINCIPLES**

## **The Vision**

| *"**Learning Without Barriers**"** — every learner and member of staff at Weston College can access, engage with, and benefit from digital learning, regardless of their ability, language, background, connectivity, or prior experience.* |
| --- |

## **Five Strategic Principles**

| **1** | **Inclusion by Design, Not Reaction** Digital inclusion must be built into every process, system, and resource from the start — not retrofitted. This applies to curriculum design, staff development, IT procurement, and student communications. |
| --- | --- |
| **2** | **Start with Our Most Excluded Learners** ESOL and SEND learners face the sharpest digital barriers. If we design our systems and resources to work for them, they will work better for everyone. These communities are our proof of concept and our conscience. |
| **3** | **Everyone is Responsible** Digital inclusion is not the responsibility of one team or role. It must be owned by the whole college — from senior leadership to teaching staff, support teams, IT, and student services. |
| **4** | **Quick Wins Drive Momentum** Transformation takes time. We will identify high-impact, low-cost actions using free tools already available to us — primarily Microsoft 365 and Jisc resources — to generate visible progress quickly and build confidence. |
| **5** | **Student Voice is Non-Negotiable** Learners must help shape this strategy — not as a tick-box exercise, but as genuine co-designers. This is particularly important for ESOL, SEND, and young learners who are most affected. |

# **SECTION 2: THE THREE PILLARS**

The strategy organises all activity into three interconnected pillars. These are not sequential — they must progress in parallel, though Pillar 1 lays the necessary foundation.

| **PILLAR 1** | **PILLAR 2** | **PILLAR 3** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Foundations** *Accessibility, Inclusion **&** Foundational Digital Skills* | **Systems ****&**** Culture** *Whole-College Digital Culture, MS Teams **&** Resource Sharing* | **Equity ****&**** Empowerment** *Student Voice, Social Justice **&** Wider Community Impact* |

## **Pillar 1: Foundations — Accessibility, Inclusion ****&**** Digital Skills**

This is the highest priority pillar and the right starting point. It focuses on building accessible, inclusive practice across the college using ESOL and SEND learners as the key quality drivers — because if our systems and resources work for them, they work for everyone.

### **1a. Accessibility as Standard Practice**

Accessibility should not be an add-on — it must be the default position for all content creation and delivery. The Jisc FE Accessibility Training Matrix (currently in Phase 2, 2025-26) provides an excellent role-mapped framework to use as our guide.

- Adopt the Jisc FE Accessibility Training Matrix as the backbone of staff development in this area

- Require all digital learning resources to pass an Accessibility Check (built into MS Word, PowerPoint and Teams — free, no training needed to start)

- Establish a minimum standard for lesson resource sharing via MS Teams, including consistent folder structure, accessible file formats, and alt text on images

- Join the Jisc Accessibility Community (free monthly drop-in clinics on the first Wednesday of each month, 12:30-13:30) — start attending immediately

### **1b. Free Microsoft Tools — Your Quick Win Arsenal**

The Microsoft 365 Education suite already available to the college contains a powerful set of free accessibility and inclusion tools. These are built-in, mainstream, and non-stigmatising. The following should be prioritised for rapid deployment:

| **Tool** | **Benefit for ESOL / SEND** | **Action to Take** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Immersive Reader** | Text-to-speech, translation into 100+ languages, text spacing/font adjustments. Embedded in Teams, Word, OneNote, Forms | Train all staff to use and demonstrate it; make it a standard part of resource inductions |
| **Live Captions ****&**** Translator (Teams)** | Real-time captions in 60+ languages. Transforms access for d/Deaf, hard of hearing, and ESOL learners in live and recorded sessions | Enable as standard in all Teams meetings; add to all lecture recordings; create a 'how to' guide for learners |
| **Accessibility Checker** | Checks all Office documents for colour contrast, alt text, reading order. Helps all learners including those with visual impairments | Build into staff CPD; add to the QA resource checklist; require for all uploaded teaching materials |
| **Dictation / Speech-to-Text** | Allows learners with limited mobility, dyslexia, or low written English to produce work via speech. Reduces assessment barriers significantly | Promote as a standard learning adjustment; share with SEND team; create learner-facing how-to cards |
| **Reading Coach** | Free AI-powered reading fluency tool used by 500k+ learners in 190+ countries. Strong evidence for reading level improvement — transformative for ESOL learners | Pilot with ESOL classes in the first term; measure reading levels before and after; use as evidence for the strategy |
| **Microsoft Translator App** | Real-time multi-device conversation translation. Bridges language barriers between learners, parents, and staff instantly in meetings or classrooms | Deploy for ESOL parent evenings and initial enrolment conversations; train student services and reception staff |

### **1c. Good Things Foundation — What We Can Take**

Good Things Foundation is the UK's leading digital inclusion charity. Their resources are free, evidence-based, and already tested in FE college settings. Their work with Newcastle College, South and City College Birmingham and others demonstrates exactly the model we should replicate.

- Learn My Way — free online digital skills platform (beginner to intermediate, multi-language), excellent for ESOL learners

- National Digital Inclusion Network — consider joining as a member to access grants, training, and the National Databank (free SIM/data for learners who cannot afford connectivity)

- Digital Champion model — train staff and peer learners as Digital Champions who support others informally

- One-page guidance cards for tutors supporting digital skills in the classroom — ready to use

- Generative AI ethical use resources (free, 8 courses) — relevant for our AI Literacy agenda

| *Action: Register Weston College with the Good Things Foundation National Digital Inclusion Network. This is free and unlocks grants, data, and resources immediately.* |
| --- |

### **1d. Jisc Resources Specifically Useful to Us**

- Jisc Building Digital Capability (BDC) Discovery Tool — staff and student digital self-assessment, includes an Accessibility & Inclusion question set. Free for FE subscribers.

- Jisc Digital Experience Insights (DEI) — surveys learners on their digital experience including accessibility. Benchmarks us against the sector.

- FE Accessibility Training Matrix — maps accessibility training to job roles across the college. Phase 2 launches 2025-26. Join as a pilot institution.

- Free monthly Accessibility Community Drop-in — first Wednesday of each month, 12:30. Register now.

- Jisc AI Literacy Framework (3 modules) — use as the basis for staff AI literacy training: Module 1 Foundation, Module 2 Applied, Module 3 Ethical.

## **Pillar 2: Systems ****&**** Culture — MS Teams, Resource Sharing ****&**** Staff Skills**

Even the best tools fail without a consistent culture of use. This pillar focuses on building the shared systems and staff confidence needed to make digital inclusion sustainable across the whole college.

### **2a. Making MS Teams Work for Everyone**

Teams is already in place, but inconsistent use creates confusion and exclusion rather than access. A whole-college standard approach is essential.

- Establish a consistent Teams channel structure across all curriculum areas — General (social), Assignments, Resources, Support

- All learning materials to be uploaded to Teams in accessible formats (Word over PDF wherever possible; PDFs must be tagged)

- Introduce a Weston College Digital Resource Quality Standard — a simple checklist that every resource must pass before upload

- Use class Teams for ESOL groups as communication hubs — including parent/guardian channels where appropriate

- Enable Live Captions and Translator as defaults for all Teams meetings college-wide

### **2b. Staff Digital Skills — Separating Learner and Staff Needs**

Staff and learner digital development needs are different and must be addressed separately.

| STAFF DIGITAL SKILLS PRIORITIES | LEARNER DIGITAL SKILLS PRIORITIES |
| --- | --- |
| Accessible resource creation (Word, PowerPoint, Teams) | Foundational digital skills (device use, internet safety, email, Teams) |
| Using Teams effectively for learning delivery and communication | Using college systems (Teams, VLE, email, assignment submission) |
| Understanding and applying Immersive Reader, Captions, Accessibility Checker | Reading and writing tools (Immersive Reader, Dictation, Reading Coach) |
| AI literacy — Module 1 Foundation Awareness (via Jisc) | Digital safety, online identity, and critical information literacy |
| Supporting SEND and ESOL learners with digital tools — confident signposting | Specific tools for ESOL: Translator, multilingual content, Reading Coach |
| Data protection, safeguarding, and professional digital communication | Job readiness: CVs, LinkedIn basics, professional email communication |

| *Key principle from DigiFest and Jisc: AI Literacy starts BEFORE the tool, not alongside it. Staff need Foundation Awareness (Module 1) before we introduce AI tools into teaching.* |
| --- |

### **2c. Quick Win Actions for Pillar 2**

- Audit current Teams usage across the college — identify inconsistencies and create a minimum standard

- Run a series of 30-minute staff workshops on the top 3 MS accessibility tools (Immersive Reader, Captions, Accessibility Checker) — maximum impact, minimum time

- Create a Weston College Digital Inclusion Moodle/Teams hub for staff — resource library, guides, and community of practice

- Use the Jisc BDC Discovery Tool to baseline staff digital confidence — this gives you data to justify further investment

- Identify 5-10 Digital Champion volunteers (staff and students) to be the peer support network

## **Pillar 3: Equity ****&**** Empowerment — Student Voice, Social Justice ****&**** Community**

This pillar addresses the longer-term, more ambitious elements of the strategy. It is worth exploring, but should build on the solid foundations of Pillars 1 and 2 rather than running ahead of them.

### **3a. Genuine Student Voice (Avoiding Youthwashing)**

A key theme from DigiFest was the danger of 'youthwashing' — surface-level involvement that generates engagement theatre without real impact. To avoid this:

- Create a student Digital Inclusion Advisory Group, with real influence over the strategy's development

- Prioritise ESOL, SEND and young learners from disadvantaged backgrounds in this group

- Co-design the learner-facing digital skills curriculum with students, not just for them

- Report back to students on what has changed as a result of their input

### **3b. Social Justice and Eco System Change — Worth Exploring?**

Your instinct to think about Social Justice and Eco System change is worth keeping in the strategy — but as a longer-term horizon, once Pillars 1 and 2 are established. Questions worth debating:

- What Social Justice and Eco System changes could Weston College advocate for or model? (e.g. lobbying for better connectivity infrastructure in the region, partnering with community organisations on digital poverty)

- Could the college become a Good Things Foundation Digital Inclusion Hub, serving not just students but the wider Weston-super-Mare community?

- How might a future Anti-Racist and Inclusive Digital Strategy connect to the college's existing EDI commitments?

| *Recommendation: Hold this pillar lightly for now. Document the questions, create space to debate them, but do not let them delay the urgent foundational work of Pillars 1 and 2.* |
| --- |

# **SECTION 3: STAKEHOLDERS ****&**** GOVERNANCE**

## **Who Needs to Be On Board**

Digital inclusion is everyone's responsibility — but it needs champions and clear ownership. Below are the key roles that need to be engaged, and why.

| **Role / Function** | **Why They Matter** | **How to Get Them On Board** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Senior Leadership Team / Principal** | Strategy ownership, budget, and policy mandate. Without SLT backing, this remains a project not a strategy. | Present this document as the case for change; link to White Paper and Ofsted inspection evidence |
| **Head of IT / Director of Digital** | Controls infrastructure, device access, and MS 365 configuration. Essential to unlock Teams features and device provision. | Share the MS tools table; frame as a quick win using existing investment |
| **SEND / Learning Support Manager** | Key ally and co-designer. SEND learners are central to the proof of concept. This is also directly relevant to White Paper SEND commitments. | Involve early; co-own the SEND elements of Pillar 1; share Jisc Accessibility Matrix |
| **ESOL Programme Leader** | ESOL learners are the highest impact test group for MS tools like Translator and Reading Coach. Their success stories are the strategy's best evidence. | Pilot the Reading Coach and Translator tools; create case studies from outcomes |
| **Head of Teaching, Learning ****&**** Assessment** | Quality framework, observation, and CPD structures. Accessibility and inclusion must become part of the TLA standards and observation criteria. | Propose adding digital accessibility to TLA observation framework; co-develop CPD programme |
| **Curriculum / Subject Heads** | Directly responsible for learning resources and Teams usage in their areas. Change only sticks when it is embedded at curriculum level. | Identify early adopters; use them as champions; work area by area, not all at once |
| **Student Services / Enrolment Team** | First contact for new learners, especially ESOL and SEND. Device and connectivity needs, and digital baseline assessments, should happen at enrolment. | Add digital baseline check to enrolment process; share Good Things Foundation National Databank with enrolment team |
| **Student Union / Student Representatives** | The voice of the student body. Essential for co-designing the strategy, especially to avoid surface-level involvement ('youthwashing'). | Create a Student Digital Inclusion Advisory Group; give it real influence and a feedback loop |
| **Marketing / Communications Team** | Must also apply accessible content standards to all college communications. External accessibility is as important as internal. | Brief the team on accessibility standards for digital communications; audit the website accessibility statement |

# **SECTION 4: PROPOSED ACTION PLAN — WHAT DO WE DO FIRST?**

This is a starting point for discussion. Actions are grouped by timeframe. The principle is: quick wins first, longer-term transformation built on solid foundations.

## **Immediate Actions (This Term — Before Easter)**

| **Action** | **Owner (Role)** | **Cost** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Register Weston College with Good Things Foundation National Digital Inclusion Network | Digital Strategy Lead | **FREE** |
| Attend first Jisc Accessibility Community Drop-in (first Wednesday of month, 12:30) | Digital Strategy Lead + SEND Manager | **FREE** |
| Pilot MS Reading Coach with one ESOL group — baseline and track reading levels | ESOL Programme Leader | **FREE** |
| Run Jisc BDC Discovery Tool accessibility question set with a sample of staff to establish baseline | Digital Strategy Lead / L&D | **FREE (Jisc member)** |
| Present this strategy document to SLT for discussion and mandate | Digital Strategy Lead | **FREE** |

## **Short-Term Actions (Summer Term)**

- Design and deliver the first 3 staff CPD sessions on MS accessibility tools (Immersive Reader, Captions, Accessibility Checker) — 30 mins each, lunch and learn format

- Establish a consistent MS Teams channel structure as the college standard — work with IT and one or two early adopter curriculum areas first

- Create the Weston College Digital Resource Quality Standard checklist — simple, one-page, built around the MS Accessibility Checker

- Launch the Student Digital Inclusion Advisory Group — recruit through ESOL, SEND and Student Union channels

- Audit the college website against the Public Sector Accessibility Regulations — is the accessibility statement up to date?

- Begin the Jisc AI Literacy Module 1 Foundation rollout for all staff — use Jisc webinar format to minimise demand on time

## **Medium-Term Actions (Next Academic Year)**

- Roll out the Jisc FE Accessibility Training Matrix across all staff roles — use the Phase 2 pilot structure

- Add digital accessibility to the TLA observation and quality assurance framework

- Launch the Good Things Foundation Digital Champion programme with trained staff and student peer supporters

- Review enrolment process to include a digital baseline check and signpost Good Things Foundation National Databank to learners without connectivity

- Commission a full Digital Experience Insights (DEI) survey via Jisc to benchmark learner and staff experience and set targets

- Begin planning for Weston College to become a registered Good Things Foundation Digital Inclusion Hub — serving the wider community

# **SECTION 5: QUESTIONS FOR DEBATE**

This document is intended to provoke discussion. The following questions are deliberately open — they do not all have right answers yet.

| *1. Do we agree that ESOL and SEND learners should be the primary lens for Pillar 1? Are there risks or opportunities we have not considered?* |
| --- |

| *2. What is the minimum standard we should set for digital accessibility of resources — and how do we enforce it without creating a culture of compliance over conviction?* |
| --- |

| *3. Who should own this strategy? Is this a Digital role, a Teaching **&** Learning role, or something that needs a new position or working group?* |
| --- |

| *4. How do we genuinely involve students — especially ESOL, SEND, and those with least digital confidence — without it becoming tokenistic?* |
| --- |

| *5. The Social Justice and Eco System pillar is the most ambitious. Is this the right moment for it, or should it wait until Pillars 1 and 2 are established? What would success even look like?* |
| --- |

| *6. What are the specific digital assumptions made about Weston College learners in the FE White Paper that worry us most — and what can we do about them right now?* |
| --- |

| *7. Are there communities in Weston-super-Mare that the college is not currently reaching who need digital inclusion support? Should the college be a community resource, not just an educational one?* |
| --- |

